# 2. WHAT IS PRIVILEGE ESCALATION?

Privilege escalation occurs when a user or process gains privileges beyond those originally authorized.

Example:

```text
Normal User
     ↓
Privilege Escalation
     ↓
Root
```

In Linux:

> **Root = UID 0**

Root has extremely broad control over the system.

---

# 3. WHY PRIVILEGE ESCALATION MATTERS

Imagine an attacker initially obtains access as:

```text
student
```

The account may have limited permissions.

However, a misconfiguration might allow:

```text
student
   ↓
sudo misconfiguration
   ↓
root
```

The impact can increase dramatically.

---

# 4. TWO MAIN TYPES

## Vertical Privilege Escalation

A lower-privileged user obtains higher privileges.

Example:

```text
Normal User
     ↓
Root
```

This is what people usually mean when discussing Linux privilege escalation.

---

## Horizontal Privilege Escalation

A user accesses another account with similar privilege.

Example:

```text
User A
  ↓
User B
```

Neither account necessarily has root privileges.

---

# 5. MEMORY TRICK

> **Vertical = Level Up**

> **Horizontal = Same Level, Different Account**

---

